00:06:07.990 --> 00:06:12.270
It’s kinda obvious that passwords are more
secure the longer they are,

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:15.580
but it can be difficult to get an intuitive
sense of just how much more secure.

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:17.699
So let’s say you’ve signed up to Dashlane,

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:21.700
and now you’re able to use long, complicated,
symbol-filled passwords everywhere

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:25.979
because Dashlane remembers,
synchronises and autofills them for you.

00:06:25.979 --> 00:06:28.350
How much more secure are those passwords?

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:29.970
Well, let’s do the maths.

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:32.740
The characters that most web sites tend to
accept for passwords are:

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:36.819
uppercase and lowercase letters, numbers,
and let’s say thirty punctuation marks.

00:06:36.819 --> 00:06:38.410
That’s 92 characters.

00:06:38.410 --> 00:06:42.949
There’s an argument that you should be allowed
to use any Unicode character in a password,

00:06:42.949 --> 00:06:44.480
but it’s definitely an argument,

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:46.930
so let’s limit it to those 92 characters.

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:50.419
First, because we’re using random characters,
we can ignore ‘wordlists’

00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:54.940
that make password cracking orders of magnitude
easier by guessing dictionary words first

00:06:54.940 --> 00:06:57.720
including, like, using an exclamation mark
instead of a 1,

00:06:57.720 --> 00:06:59.919
or deliberately swapping a couple of letters.

00:06:59.919 --> 00:07:01.330
Those tricks won’t work here.

00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:03.820
Now, let’s say that an attacker
is able to test

00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:06.700
a billion possible passwords
every single second.

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:10.159
That’s not unreasonable if the encryption
is weak and their computer is fast.

00:07:10.159 --> 00:07:11.759
If you have a six-character password,

00:07:11.759 --> 00:07:14.349
it will take them at 
about ten minutes to try

00:07:14.349 --> 00:07:18.610
every single possible one of the
606 billion combinations.

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:21.800
But every letter you add
multiples that time by 92.

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:24.259
Seven characters works out to
15 and a half hours.

00:07:24.259 --> 00:07:25.819
Eight characters, about two months.

00:07:25.819 --> 00:07:28.169
Nine characters, 14 years.

00:07:28.169 --> 00:07:30.880
Ten characters, more than a millennium.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:33.150
Eleven characters is a hundred thousand years.

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:34.650
By the time you’ve got to fifteen characters,

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:38.530
it’s about a thousand times longer than
the universe has existed.

00:07:38.530 --> 00:07:42.770
Long, complicated, random passwords aren’t
a cure-all.

00:07:42.770 --> 00:07:45.370
But they’re certainly better than the alternative.

